Cat/dog teaching dataset
========================

Source: Jacquemont, Mikaël (2021), “Cats and Dogs sample,” Zenodo, version 1,
https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5226945. This is a 32,608,921-byte archive with
1,000 train and 400 test images (balanced 500/500 and 200/200), described by its
creator as a small sample of the Kaggle Dogs vs. Cats dataset. The archive is
downloaded on demand; the builder uses only 64 images per class.

The Zenodo record marks the deposited dataset CC BY 4.0. Attribution is
required; redistributed or adapted images must retain appropriate credit and
indicate changes. The source archive checksum (MD5) is checked by the builder.

The Zenodo subset does not contain enough usable images for 2,048 samples.
Inspection found 639 cat and 728 dog files with minimum dimension >=128px
(591 cats / 683 dogs >=200px). The builder therefore uses the allowed 1,024
fallback: first 512 qualifying images per class after lexical source-path sort.
The released sample is light/downscaled, with original sides ranging from 49 to
500px; selected images are all at least 128px in each dimension.

This replaces the former VOC/bounding-box pipeline. Each image gets a
deterministic largest centered square crop. Manifest `crop_rect` records
`[x, y, width, height]` in original source pixels. Crops are JPEGs and remain
generated artifacts, not committed source data.

Run `./build_dataset.sh` from the repository root. The script creates/uses a
virtual environment, installs Pillow from `requirements.txt`, downloads the
32.6 MB archive, and writes 1,024 crops and `data/manifest.json`: 512 cats,
512 dogs; per class 384 train, 64 val, and 64 holdout. Selection and split are
deterministic: source paths sort lexically per class, then samples are assigned
sequentially to the splits.
